sentences.l:
So they by the curved ships were arming themselves
around you, son of Peleus, insatiate of battle, the Achaeans,
and the Trojans, in turn, on the other side, on the rising ground of the plain;

Hera, for one, to the gathering of the ships, and Pallas Athena,
and Poseidon, the earth-holder, and the helper
Hermes, who in keen wits excels;

As long as the gods were apart from mortal men,
so long the Achaeans exulted greatly, because Achilles
had appeared, and for long from grievous fighting had ceased;

For indeed, opposite Poseidon the lord
stood Apollo Phoebus, holding winged arrows,
and opposite Enyalius the goddess, gleaming-eyed Athena;

but Achilles
opposite Hector most of all longed to plunge into the throng,
the son of Priam;

sentences.t:
Αἰνεία Τρώων βουληφόρε ποῦ τοι ἀπειλαὶ
ἃς Τρώων βασιλεῦσιν ὑπίσχεο οἰνοποτάζων
Πηλεΐδεω Ἀχιλῆος ἐναντίβιον πολεμίξειν;

sentences.tr:
Aineia Trōōn boulēphore pou toi apeilai
has Trōōn basileusin hypischeo oinopotazōn
Pēleïdeō Achilēos enantibion polemixein;

sentences.l:
For not now for the first time against fleet-footed Achilles
shall I stand; no, already at another time he put me to flight with his spear
from Ida, when he came upon our cattle,
and sacked Lyrnessus and Pedasus;
